## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic triangle counting with tight amortized analysis, batching, and memory trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic graphs, incremental triangles
- Degree-based edge orientation, forward neighbor sets
- Amortized analysis, batch processing, space efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
// high-level sketch
const Nplus = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> new Set());
const deg = new Int32Array(n);
let triangles = 0;
function addEdge(u,v){
  // decide orientation
  if (deg[u] > deg[v] || (deg[u]===deg[v] && u>v)) [u,v] = [v,u];
  // count new triangles
  let t = 0;
  for (let w of Nplus[u]) if (Nplus[v].has(w)) t++;
  triangles += t;
  Nplus[u].add(v);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
  // potentially rebalance orientation lazily
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does the method adapt if deletions are allowed?
- What are practical bottlenecks on real graphs (power-law networks)?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to combine streaming sketches with efficient top-K data structures while reasoning about accuracy, space, and time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- **Streaming Algorithms:** Processing unbounded data with limited memory
- **Count-Min Sketch (CMS):** Probabilistic data structure for frequency estimation with false positives but no false negatives
- **Top-K Maintenance:** Using a min-heap to track the K most frequent elements efficiently
- **Amortized Analysis:** Bounding average cost over sequences of operations
- **Space-Time Trade-offs:** Comparing approximate vs. exact approaches

## Code Example
```javascript
class CountMinSketch {
  constructor(w, d) {
    this.w = w;
    this.d = d;
    this.table = Array.from({ length: d }, () => Array(w).fill(0));
  }

  hash(key, i) {
    const str = typeof key === 'string' ? key : String(key);
    let hash = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < str.length; j++) {
      hash = (hash * 31 + str.charCodeAt(j)) >>> 0;
    }
    return (hash + i * 31) % this.w;
  }

  update(key) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      this.table[i][j]++;
    }
  }

  estimate(key) {
    let min = Infinity;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      min = Math.min(min, this.table[i][j]);
    }
    return min;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question explores advanced incremental data-structure design under streaming constraints, requiring mastery of amortized analysis for batched updates alongside exact substring query capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- **Suffix Automaton fundamentals**: States, transitions, links, and the endpos equivalence relation
- **Batched extension strategies**: Buffering B characters before committing to the global structure
- **Clone management and amortized cost**: Deferred clone creation and lazy propagation techniques
- **Transition representations**: Compact maps vs. dense arrays for cache locality
- **Trade-off analysis**: Balancing batch size B, alphabet size σ, and total string length N

## Code Example

```cpp
struct State {
    int link;
    int len;
    std::unordered_map<char, int> next;  // or vector<int> for small σ
};

class BatchedSuffixAutomaton {
    std::vector<State> st;
    int last;
    std::string buffer;
    const size_t B;

public:
    BatchedSuffixAutomaton(size_t batch_size = 1024) : B(batch_size), last(0) {
        st.push_back({-1, 0, {}});
    }

    void extend(char c) {
        buffer.push_back(c);
        if (buffer.size() < B) return;
        flushBuffer();
    }

    void flushBuffer() {
        for (char c : buffer) {
            int cur = st.size();
            st.push_back({0, st[last].len + 1, {}});
            int p = last;
            while (p >= 0 && !st[p].next.count(c)) {
                st[p].next[c] = cur;
                p = st[p].link;
            }
            if (p == -1) {
                st[cur].link = 0;
            } else {
                int q = st[p].next[c];
                if (st[p].len + 1 == st[q].len) {
                    st[cur].link = q;
                } else {
                    int clone = st.size();
                    st.push_back(st[q]);  // clone
                    st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
                    while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] == q) {
                        st[p].next[c] = clone;
                        p = st[p].link;
                    }
                    st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
                }
            }
            last = cur;
        }
        buffer.clear();
    }

    bool query(const std::string& p) {
        int v = 0;
        for (char c : p) {
            if (!st[v].next.count(c)) return false;
            v = st[v].next[c];
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

## Optimal Batch Size

The optimal B depends on workload characteristics:
- **Small σ (e.g., DNA, σ ≤ 4)**: Larger B (4096+) works well; dense transition arrays gain from cache locality
- **Large σ (e.g., Unicode, σ ≈ 10⁵)**: Smaller B (128–512); sparse maps reduce memory pressure
- **Memory-constrained environments**: B ≈ √N balances update frequency against per-batch overhead
- **Latency-sensitive queries**: Smaller B reduces worst-case query delay between flushes

## Why This Is Asked
Explores practical dynamic approximate all-pairs shortest paths (APSP) with shared structure; tests understanding of trade-offs between update costs and query time in batch-dynamic settings.

## Key Concepts
- Batch-dynamic graphs with nonnegative edge weights
- Hub-based distance sketches for approximate APSP
- Amortized analysis across multiple query pairs
- Trade-offs between approximation accuracy ε and computational efficiency
- Incremental shortest path algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
// Hub-based distance approximation: dist(u,v) ≈ min_h∈H [dist(u,h) + dist(h,v)]

class HubBasedAPSP {
  constructor(graph, epsilon) {
    this.k = Math.ceil((1/epsilon) * Math.log(graph.n));
    this.hubs = this.selectHubs(graph);
    this.distances = new Map(); // hub -> vertex distances
  }

  updateBatch(edges) {
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      this.distances.set(hub, this.incrementalDijkstra(hub, edges));
    }
  }

  query(u, v) {
    let minDist = Infinity;
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      const dist = this.distances.get(hub).get(u) + 
                   this.distances.get(hub).get(v);
      minDist = Math.min(minDist, dist);
    }
    return minDist;
  }
}
```

